  "title": "Universal point sets for planar graphs",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "We say that a set $P \\subseteq {\\mathbb R}^2$ is $n$-universal if every $n$ vertex planar graph can be drawn in the plane so that each vertex maps to a distinct point in $P$, and all edges are (non-intersecting) straight line segments.\n\nQuestion Does there exist an $n$-universal set of size $O(n)$?",
